# Review package: batch 4, slice delta

## cc742d93 Count IPv4 visitors by their address, read a long challenge token, and say nothing false about an account

The fix round of batch 4 on sign-in limits, the TLS listener and ACME, from its one review (runs/batch4-sec-merged.md).

addressKey now parses a colon address into its eight groups once. An IPv4-mapped address in any form counts by its
IPv4, others by the /64. readProxyV2 writes family 0x21 as eight plain groups, which the old pattern did not know, so
every such visitor shared 0:0:0:0::/64 with ::1, and one stranger's 30 failures locked all of them out for an hour.

The challenge route is a wildcard, with TOKEN as its bound. Fastify's maxParamLength of 100 sits under TOKEN's 128, so a
token of 101 to 128 characters got index.html from the web app and issuance failed.

account() gives back a URL it already holds and asks the CA nothing, so it no longer says a stored URL is good when a
swallowed update said otherwise. A URL got in this very call still ignores the follow-up update's failure, and that
failure leaves no lastProblem for a later local failure to read as its own. A failure of the create itself is thrown as
before. issue() now calls account() as it is, since the check for a held URL lives there alone.

Tests. The seam test of the listener and the limit of 30 per address replaces its it.todo. A new test reads pg_locks at
each try's insert: the lock is still held there, and gone after. On PGlite's one connection the 100-at-once test passes
with db.transaction removed, and this one does not, which is what holds the limit on a pool. A real race waits for plan
3's Postgres harness; the note is beside the test.

While there: longestWait seeds from the first wait, so a 60 minute limit in its last second does not say 15 minutes.
retryAfterSeconds hands Date.parse only the form of an HTTP date, so "-5" and "retry 10" read as no header and not as no
wait. problemOf's content type is case-insensitive. closedPort is one function for its two test files.

Correction to ceb0e8a3: its message says the limits on sign-ins and invites live in the database. Only sign-ins moved;
invites stay on the in-memory limiter.

Proof: each new test seen failing for its reason first (0:0:0:0::/64; text/html for the token; /account/1 asked; refused
with malformed in place of unreachable; 15 minutes in place of 60). The tests that pinned code already right were run
against it broken: the seam test fails when clientAddress ignores the listener's visitor and when any address is
believed, the lock test fails with the transaction removed, and the token tests fail with TOKEN's bound removed.

Ruling: the one transaction is shown by asking pg_locks at each insert on its own connection handle, not by spying what opens the transaction - why: the database's own word tells a lock still held from one already gone, where a count of opened transactions says nothing of what ran inside them; it catches a removed transaction, a lock moved to another one and a missing lock - cost if wrong: the test reads one more thing of PGlite's, and plan 3's real race covers it anyway
Ruling: closedPort lives in test/closed-port.ts, not in helpers.ts - why: a change in helpers.ts makes the author check run the whole server package, the lead's condition for moving it - cost if wrong: one more small test file
Ruling: retryAfterSeconds reads an HTTP date only in IMF-fixdate, not RFC 9110's two obsolete forms - why: it is the form CAs send, a shape check is what keeps nonsense from Date.parse, and the asctime form would read in the machine's time zone - cost if wrong: a CA that sends an obsolete form gets the default wait in place of its own
Ruling: every failure of the follow-up update is ignored when this call got the URL, a 5xx or no answer too, not only a refusal - why: this is the writer's way the lead kept, the URL is in hand, and the next order meets the same CA - cost if wrong: the same failure is told one request later by createOrder

