## 78c0c7e2 Keep a removed node from revoking its own new key, and lose no node message on a reconnect or a full disk

Batch 4's one fix round on the node's messages, runs/batch4-node-merged.md. Each fix came from a test that failed first.

A removed in the outbox outlived the removal: set-key brought the node back, the node replayed it after hello and wiped its
new key. set-key now clears the outbox when the node was removed, or its server or subscription changed. A socket drop while
remove waited lost the word for good, since stop() closed the connection at once and Hornhoof kept the key of a node that was
gone. The node now stays connected until removed is confirmed, was sent to a server that confirms nothing, or its key is
refused, which is how a server that took removed and closed the socket answers the next connection.

A hello past 100 runs or parts was dropped by the server, and the node got no more work. The protocol's bound goes up to
HELLO_IDS_MAX, and helloIds caps at 100 for an older server, the runs still going first.

runOne and testOne held an end a second time, so an end that a new connection sent and Hornhoof confirmed while the limits were
read went out again under a new number. They now number what is held, and nothing when none is.

sendPending dropped what it never sent on an older server; it now drops up to the last that really went. A failed write left a
file with no stream line, or a line cut short that spoiled the next one, and a restart then lost messages. Both catches now mark
the file dirty, so it is rewritten whole from memory. flush() gets its test across two drop cycles with no close.

Skills followed: test-driven-development, systematic-debugging, verification-before-completion.

Ruling: HELLO_IDS_MAX is 20,000, the outbox's own cap - why: a node never drops an end, so the bound only has to stay far above what a node can owe, and both lists at that length leave half of the socket's 4 MiB for the rest of the hello - cost if wrong: an end past the bound is not listed, so the hello gives its job up as machine_restarted.
Ruling: finding 4 is reproduced with a next() that stops taking messages, directly on sendPending, moved to outbox.ts for that - why: a real socket does not change state inside the synchronous flush of onOpen, so no socket test can show it - cost if wrong: none, the fix only drops less.
Ruling: finding 6 has no code to fail against, so its test was proved by breaking flush() by hand two ways (timer left set, no rewrite); both failed it, then flush() was restored - why: flush() has no bug, the finding is a missing test - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: a node that restarts while removed still waits stays idle, and does not say it again - why: the list fixes the live connection, and a removed flag still means idle at start; the server already gives such a node no work, because its subscription's removedAt stops the hello - cost if wrong: Hornhoof keeps that node's key until the machine is set up again, as for any node that never comes back.
Ruling: set-key also clears the outbox when node.json is missing or unreadable - why: nothing then says whose its messages were, and an old removed must not reach a new key - cost if wrong: a node whose node.json broke loses what waited when it is set up again.
Ruling: the removal's wait is tested against a fake server only - why: the server part of plan 4 Task 4 (acks, the hub) is not in this lane - cost if wrong: if that server part neither confirms removed nor refuses the key after it, the node stays connected, idle, until Docker stops it.

